Core Concept
Assassin Order Simulator — an open-world pixel-style survival-craft-build management sim: gathering, building, recruiting, crafting weapons/poison, and taking commissions that escalate from small jobs to history-changing contracts.

Story Premise
The MC is a disabled crown prince/princess (blind, with a limp) banished after a coup. They flee with one loyal servant and build an assassin guild in secret to destroy the dynasty that took everything. The MC's divine gift is clairvoyant sight within limited range, and the power to forge a life-contract with masters — compelling absolute loyalty ("cannot betray, must protect with their lives") that lasts until the MC's death. Since the main story doesn't kill the MC off, every recruited master is effectively permanent. The MC operates as a hidden leader; only masters know their true identity, and the first master (the loyal servant) becomes their eyes, ears, and hands.

Core Mechanics
Dual-mode actions: assign an assassin (idle/timer, safe but slow) or go personally via master (active, risky — failure benches that master temporarily)
Masters: gacha-collectible, one on-duty at a time, passive skill + active mission kit
Assassins: plentiful roster, simpler stats, specialties (poison, ranged, infiltration, brute)
World: zone-based overworld for mobile feasibility, but open enough for freeform building anywhere on the map (Medieval Dynasty–style) — including outside the main castle

Anti-Monotony Strategy
Freedom of base identity/specialization (poison-guild vs. weapon-guild vs. self-sufficient) gives macro purpose
Story chapters arrive as patch-based branches in new zones — each branch has a genuinely different core mechanic/mini-game (Arknights-event style), not just reskinned systems, while sharing a stable "core grammar" (gather → produce → deliver) so players don't relearn the game each time
Branches require assigning a master away from the main castle; branch resources feed back into the main castle and unlock higher-tier commissions; the main castle expands as branches develop
Branch order is fixed/authored, not player-chosen, tied to story progression

Scattered Open-World Building
Players can build anywhere on the map (like hunting lodges in Medieval Dynasty), but placement is a real strategic decision — bad spots risk discovery and destruction by the ruling authority. This reinforces both the "spatial management" layer and the hidden-rebellion narrative theme. Still to define: what makes a spot good/risky, warning system before destruction, stakes when a base is lost, and rewards for long-term successful concealment.

Retention Philosophy
Explicitly not chasing daily-quest-driven login habits. Instead aiming for "I belong there" motivation — a persistent, living world worth checking on (ambient changes, visible ownership/growth, character moments) rather than reward-gated tasks. Scoped as an indie project: "good enough" for solid monetization and experience, not competing with AAA live-service retention systems.

Freedom-Driven Emergent Gameplay
Open-ended systems naturally produce funny/emergent player stories — a "master assassin guild" that's secretly a farm, attachment/jokes around specific masters, deliberately bad base placements as challenge runs, solo/minimalist playstyles. This works because narratively "serious" characters (bound-by-contract operatives) have total mechanical freedom to be redirected — the gap between intended and player-chosen role is the comedy.
 
Alchemy, Seed Exploration, Smithing
Alchemy (unknown-recipe herb crafting) and seed exploration (personal master forest exploration, plant-to-reveal) were discussed as deep optional systems tied to the farmer identity. Smithing (tool tiers gating efficiency, 2048-style weapon merge-crafting) was recommended as the one deep system to fully build for v1, with alchemy/seed-exploration deferred to post-launch.
 
Money & Black Market Economy
Money is a universal-but-imperfect equalizer — anything can be bought, but the market buys cheap/sells expensive, and rare purchases carry detection risk tied to the concealment/authority theme. Risk consequence: master imprisonment. Refined into a heat/suspicion meter (systemic, not per-purchase gambling) rather than flat dice rolls.
 
Master Economic Specialization (Gacha Hook)
Black-market price/risk bonuses gated behind master skills obtainable via gacha, giving SSR (or scaled R/SR/SSR) masters value as economic specialists — a new gacha-pull axis beyond combat, reinforcing "different identities, all viable."
 
V1 Scope Definition
Decided v1 should include all core systems except the branch/patch system, sequenced naturally through the story: survival start → hut → hideout → recruiting → minor commissions → growing guild → climax commission. Specifically excavation/mining, farming, and smithing (capped at mid-tier) are v1 systems; alchemy/poison is explicitly deferred to the branch/patch system, not launch.
 
V1 Story Arc
MC (disabled royal in hiding) starts alone with one master, survives, builds a hut → hideout, recruits assassins, takes minor commissions against commoner targets, escalating to a climax commission: assassinating a corrupt town/village head with a dangerous agenda to destroy the town. Ending: the head is replaced with an ally of the assassin order, making the main castle's surrounding area safe from authority detection going forward — a satisfying resolution that also sets up branches as where risk/tension re-escalates in new zones.
 
Climax Item: The Obsidian Blade
The climax commission requires an obsidian blade, obtainable through two (now three) paths:
1. Craft it — via committed mining + smithing investment (mid-tier crafting payoff)
2. Buy it — attempting this on the black market fails: the seller refuses and reports the player, resulting in the manager being detained (not just risked) — a scripted betrayal beat that punishes trying to "buy your way out," consistent with the story's theme of corrupt shortcuts. but player can buy the resources to craft the blade.
3. Broker it via diplomacy — training/using a high-skill (ideally SSR) diplomat master allows successfully negotiating the purchase instead, giving money/social-focused players a legitimate third path without being forced into crafting
 
This makes the climax a genuine convergence point: every playstyle needs some real investment (crafting mastery or diplomatic mastery) to succeed, rather than money alone trivializing the final challenge — preserving the "money can't solve everything" rule at the moment it matters most.
 
Recap: What's confirmed for v1
- Core survival/building loop (hut → hideout → castle, free placement)
- Excavation/mining (dual-mode: personal master vs. assigned assassin)
- Farming (basic resources; seed exploration optional/discovery-based, without alchemy ties yet)
- Smithing + 2048-style weapon merge-crafting (capped at mid-tier, with obsidian as a special climax-tier exception)
- Money & black market economy, including heat/suspicion risk and master imprisonment consequence
- Master gacha system, including at least one economic-specialist (negotiation/diplomacy) skill line
- Full story arc from solo survival through the corrupt town head climax
- Explicitly excluded from v1: alchemy/poison crafting, branch/patch zones — both reserved for post-launch content
